        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Machine learning models continuously grow in size and require ever more data to improve. This paradigm of scaling has lead to significant improvements in a multitude of applications, but it also exerts an ever increasing toll on privacy. To train large models, data has to be collected and centralized by single parties, for example, tech companies with large enough user bases. As such, these entities are interested in collecting as much data as possible and in this way extract value from their users. Moreover, once data is collected, it is rarely erased. This is fundamentally in conflict with commonly held notions of privacy.\nAgainst this backdrop, federated learning has emerged as the field of core technologies aiming to train machine learning models with decentralized data, that is without the need for a central party to collect all data. By exchanging model updates instead of data, user devices update a centralized model without ever giving up control of their data. This concept has made federated learning (FL) a popular direction for the future of distributed computing with privacy. In many applications, federated learning is slower than centralized training (Bonawitz et al., 2019), but benefits to privacy may outweigh these limitations, especially in applications like next-word keyboard prediction that handle all text that a user might type in a smartphone (Hard et al., 2019).\nIn turn, this expectation of privacy through federated learning is sometimes taken for granted. In reality, the actual privacy achieved by federated learning systems depends on a large number of factors and parameters - model sizes, architectures, user numbers, aggregation amounts, among others. Attacks against privacy in federated learning probe this boundary, empirically discovering which federated learning scenarios are unsafe (Phong et al., 2017; Melis et al., 2019; Geiping et al., 2020).\nIn this work, we are particularly interested in federated learning systems involving transformer architectures (Vaswani et al., 2017) which form the backbone of many recent breakthroughs with large models (Brown et al., 2020; Dosovitskiy et al., 2021; Jumper et al., 2021), and especially applications in text, which represent a key point of interest in many modern applications of federated learning (Paulik et al., 2021; Dimitriadis et al., 2022). Our main threat models of interest here are\nuntrusted server scenarios, also known as malicious server scenarios, contrasting themselves with investigations in honest-but-curious scenarios, which limit the extent of possible attacks. Untrusted server scenarios are of critical importance from a user-centric privacy perspective. If a federated learning system is supposed to uphold privacy, then ideally this privacy can be guaranteed without having to assert full trust in the server. After all, a perfectly trustworthy server could run the simplest FL protocol: Download all user data, promise not to copy it, train a centralized model, and delete all data. Another way to look at untrusted server scenarios is to see them as a worst-case scenario. Even if we believe a server will uphold privacy, we might wonder about the worst-case loss in privacy that would arise if this server is even briefly compromised (through either classical security breaches or poisoning attacks (Bagdasaryan et al., 2019)) and acts maliciously.\nA central idea for such a malicious server is to modify the current state of the partially trained machine learning model that is shared with the users. What is the worst-case model state that a malicious server can send to users? As this model directly operates on user data according to the chosen FL protocol, this can be understood as an analogue to untrusted code instructions that are being evaluated on a user\u2019s private data (OWASP, 2022; Fowl et al., 2022).\nIn this work, we discuss a novel attack threat where a malicious server is able to extract key fragments of private information even from industrial-sized streams of data. This novel attack, which we dub \u201cpanning\u201d as it sifts through large amounts of text, is capable of targeting key phrases, such as credit card or social security number and extracting all tokens of user data that follow the occurrence of this trigger. This is an essential shift in capabilities for attacks against transformer-based model in federated learning. Existing attacks only recover user data - no matter how private or benign - in scenarios where the number of model parameters is significantly smaller than the number of tokens in a user update (Fowl et al., 2022; Gupta et al., 2022; Dimitrov et al., 2022; Pasquini et al., 2021). In some cases, attacks can siphon random examples of user data but only through a large number of repeated queries (Wen et al., 2022). In contrast, the discussed attack has the capability to target key phrases of private user data and recover sensitive text, e.g. credit card or social security numbers, or any other secrets, following these triggers. In comparison to existing attacks, this attack is also effectively independent of the amount of user data contained in an update and does not degrade as many user updates are securely aggregated (Bonawitz et al., 2017)."
